<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gustav Mahler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mahler</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gustav Mahler was an Austrian composer and conductor, known for his large-scale symphonies and song cycles. He served as director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> Court Opera. He is counted among the figures of the cultural flowering of Vienna under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler.jpg" alt="Gustav Mahler" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moritz Nähr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photo_of_Gustav_Mahler_by_Moritz_N%C3%A4hr_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vienna's fin-de-siècle cultural flowering was inseparable from this prosperity: the Ringstrasse, the Secession movement, coffee-house intellectual life and the professional class that produced Sigmund Freud, <b>Gustav Mahler</b>, Gustav Klimt and Theodor Herzl were all products of a prosperous, cosmopolitan urban economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1898 <b>Gustav Mahler</b> set the Wunderhorn text for voice and orchestra.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-gedanken-sind-frei">Die Gedanken sind frei</a></li><li><blockquote>Liszt, Saint-Saëns, <b>Mahler</b>, Mussorgsky, Tchaikovsky and Rachmaninoff, in more than a dozen works, quoted it, and it has been used in many film scores and popular works, from the score for Metropolis (1927) and Citizen Kane (1941) to The Shining (1980) and The Lion King.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gustav Mahler</b> used it, in a minor key and under its German name &quot;Bruder Martin&quot;, in the third movement of his Symphony No.</blockquote><a href="/songs/frere-jacques">Frère Jacques</a></li><li><blockquote>…tune for four centuries: Jehan Titelouze (1623), Marc-Antoine Charpentier (five settings), Johann Pachelbel, Nicolas de Grigny, Johann Sebastian Bach (the chorale BWV 370 and the organ preludes BWV 631 and 667), Berlioz, Franck, Bruckner, <b>Gustav Mahler</b>, who set the Latin text in Part I of his Symphony No.</blockquote><a href="/songs/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus</a></li></ul></section>
